// Logos standard library — logos.mem.any.anybox
//
// `AnyBox` — an OWNING type-erased value, and the Writ case of it.
//
// ── Why this is a new type and not an extension of something ──────────────────
// `logos.lang.any` ships the `Any` TRAIT (blanket `impl<T> Any for T`) plus
// `&dyn Any` downcasting. There is nothing there that HOLDS a value: the owning
// form is `Box<dyn Any>`, and that is unported — `Box::new(x)` builds and
// dispatches, but reborrowing it to `&dyn Any` (the only route to
// `downcast_ref`/`any_is`) is refused by mlir-gen with "no vtable for '&dyn Any'
// as '&dyn Any' — the fat pointer's vtable half would be left uninitialised".
// The same gap is recorded at `panic.logos` ("richer Any-based capture waits on a
// Box<dyn Any> port") and in the any test harness. So the owning container is a
// concrete type over a CLOSED payload set (the Logos scalars + a Writ document),
// not a universal `Box<dyn Any>`. When the `&*b` reborrow lands, this becomes a
// thin specialisation over it rather than a parallel mechanism.
//
// ── The Writ case: ownership by owning ────────────────────────────────────────
// A `WAny` is an 8-byte handle whose Ref arm points INTO a Writ arena; it is not
// an owner and it is `#[borrow_carrying]`, so it cannot be stored next to the
// arena it borrows (`struct { arena: Writ, root: WAny }` is rejected: "cannot move
// 'h' while it is borrowed"). Two live problems follow from that: a `WAny` string
// is arena-interned and cannot be substituted for a borrowed view, and
// `WAny::ref_to` on a stack struct compiles while `type_code()` reads whatever
// byte precedes the value.
//
// `AnyBox` answers both by OWNING: it clones the value out of its source arena
// into a FRESH arena it owns, and the root lives in that arena's own root word
// (Writ's existing idiom — `set_root`/`root()`), so only the `Writ` is moved and
// no `#[borrow_carrying]` handle is stored. Lifetime and provenance stop being
// questions: every reference handed out is `&'a T` tied to `&'a self`.
//
// ── THE DESIGN POINT: TypeId reports the PAYLOAD, never the wrapper ───────────
// An `AnyBox` built from a Writ `i64` must be indistinguishable from one built
// from a plain `i64` — same `TypeId`, same downcast. If it reported "a Writ
// container", every consumer would have to know about Writ to get at the value,
// and the extension would buy nothing.
//
// Here that holds BY CONSTRUCTION, not by a `TypeId` coincidence: a Writ SCALAR is
// NORMALISED at construction — decoded to its native Logos type and stored in the
// owned inline slot, with the originating Writ code deliberately DISCARDED
// (`writ_code()` reports 0). The two boxes are byte-identical but for the address
// of their empty arenas. That is the stronger property, and it is the one the
// gate asserts.
//
// Normalisation is not optional cosmetics. `Writ::int` routes every value fitting
// 56 bits to a Pod, where the payload lives in bits[63:8] of a word — THERE IS NO
// NATIVE `i64` IN MEMORY to hand a `&i64` to. Pointing into the arena works only
// for the BOXED scalars and fails silently for exactly the common case.
//
//   Writ code                    → payload type      form
//   WA_I56 (1), W_I64 (26)       → i64               scalar (normalised)
//   WA_BOOL (2)                  → bool              scalar
//   WT_I8/U8/I16/U16/I24/U24     → i8/u8/i16/u16/i24/u24  scalar
//   W_U64 (27)                   → u64               scalar
//   W_F32 (30) / W_F64 (31)      → f32 / f64         scalar
//   W_STRING (130)               → WString           writ (in the owned arena)
//   W_ARRAY (100)                → WArray<WAny>      writ
//   W_MAP (101)                  → WMap<WString,WAny> writ
//   everything else              → WritOpaque        writ
//
// ── The aggregate case, and why it is neither per-kind ids nor a refusal ──────
// A Writ array/map/string HAS a Logos type to name — the view type that already
// reads it — so it gets that type's real `TypeId` and a real, sound downcast:
// `downcast_ref::<WArray<WAny>>()` yields a `&WArray<WAny>` into the owned arena,
// borrow-tied to `&self`. For a typed dense array, an int-keyed dense map, a
// TypedValue, a decimal or a tiny map there is no such type; those get ONE
// reserved structural id, `type_id_of::<WritOpaque>()`, and are reached with
// `as_writ()` + `writ_code()`.
//
// REFUSING those was the alternative and it is wrong: it would make `AnyBox`
// unable to hold most Writ documents, which is the entire point of the exercise.
// A reserved id per kind was the other alternative and buys nothing — it
// multiplies ids while still naming no downcastable Logos type, and `writ_code()`
// already gives the consumer that discrimination exactly and losslessly.
//
// ── What it costs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A scalar `AnyBox` still carries a `Writ` field (the struct has one layout), so
// it pays one `writ_new(0)` — a single small malloc, freed on drop. It is not
// null-able without making `allocator_alloc` fault on a future in-place mutation,
// and correctness beat the malloc.
//
// ── WHY THIS IS IN `mem` AND NOT IN `lang.any` ───────────────────────────────
// On the merits: `logos.lang.any` is Writ-free, and it is the package every user
// of `Any`/`TypeId` imports; putting a Writ-walking owner in the same archive
// hands that dependency to all of them. Every other consumer of the Writ walkers
// (`mem.writ.clone`, `mem.writ.equal`, `mem.writ.stringify`) already lives here.
//
// ⚠ BUT THE PLACEMENT IS ALSO LOAD-BEARING FOR THE WRONG REASON, so read this
// before moving it. Written as `logos.lang.any.anybox` it turned
// `tests/logos/pass/wany_niche_enum.logos` RED:
//   mlir-gen: duplicate function body for symbol 'WArray$G1$WAny$M…__push'
// That test declares its OWN `#[zoned] enum WAny`. A mangled name renders a type
// ARGUMENT by NAME, and the archived stdlib bodies are re-parsed in the consumer's
// scope, so `WArray<WAny>` from the stdlib and `WArray<WAny>` over the consumer's
// enum collapse onto one symbol. MEASURED PRE-EXISTING: the same failure
// reproduces on the UNMODIFIED main-repo build through the long-shipped
// `logos.mem.writ.clone::writ_clone`, from any consumer that declares a local
// `WAny` (probe: /home/logos/sandbox/probe/shadow2.logos). Moving the package to
// `mem` did NOT fix that — it only changed which consumers reach it; a program
// that imports THIS package and declares its own `WAny` still fails
// (probe: shadow.logos). Same class as the 2026-08-05 commit e07fcde4 ("an enum's
// type arguments are part of its name where the name is re-parsed as source").
// The fix is in the mangler, not here.

package logos.mem.any.anybox;

use logos.lang.any;               // TypeId, type_id_of
use logos.lang.num.float;         // f32_to_bits / f64_to_bits
use logos.lang.option;            // Option
use logos.lang.result;            // Result
use logos.lang.writ.container;    // Writ, writ_new
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;       // WAny, W_* / WA_* / WT_* codes
use logos.lang.writ.array;        // WArray<WAny>
use logos.lang.writ.wmap;         // WMap<WString, WAny>
use logos.lang.writ.wstring;      // WString
use logos.lang.writ.compactify;   // try_clone, CloneRefusal

/// `AnyBox::form` — the payload is a native Logos scalar in the inline slot.
pub const ANYBOX_SCALAR: i64 = 0i64;
/// `AnyBox::form` — the payload is a Writ value living in the box's own arena.
pub const ANYBOX_WRIT: i64 = 1i64;

/// The reserved payload type for a Writ value no Logos type names — a typed dense
/// array, an int-keyed dense map, a `TypedValue`, a decimal, a tiny map.
///
/// It is a marker: an `AnyBox` reporting `type_id_of::<WritOpaque>()` never
/// downcasts to a Logos type; read it with `as_writ()` and discriminate on
/// `writ_code()`, which carries the exact wire code losslessly.
pub struct WritOpaque { }

/// An owning, type-erased value: a native Logos scalar, or a Writ value cloned into
/// an arena this box owns.
///
/// `type_id()` always names the PAYLOAD's type, never the wrapper's — an `AnyBox`
/// over a Writ `i64` is indistinguishable from one over a plain `i64`. Every
/// reference it hands out is borrow-tied to `&self`, so provenance and lifetime are
/// settled by ownership rather than by convention.
pub struct AnyBox {
    // The PAYLOAD's type — i64 for a Writ i56 Pod just as for a plain i64.
    tid:  TypeId,
    // ANYBOX_SCALAR | ANYBOX_WRIT.
    form: i64,
    // For ANYBOX_WRIT, the originating Writ wire code (the only discriminator for a
    // WritOpaque payload). DELIBERATELY 0 for a normalised scalar: keeping it would
    // make a box built from a Writ i64 distinguishable from one built from a plain
    // i64, which is exactly the property this type exists to provide.
    code: u64,
    // The native scalar payload, in the LOW bytes (little-endian): `&bits` is the
    // `&T` a scalar downcast hands out, so an f32 occupies the low 4 bytes, a bool
    // the low 1, and an i8 is stored sign-extended.
    bits: u64,
    // The owned arena. For ANYBOX_WRIT it holds the cloned value, reachable through
    // its own root word (`root()`); for ANYBOX_SCALAR it is empty and exists only
    // because the struct has one layout.
    arena: Writ,
}

// Default arena segment size for a Writ payload. Sized so an ordinary document
// lands in a single segment; `of_writ_in` overrides it.
const ANYBOX_SEG: i64 = 4096i64;

// ── construction from a native Logos scalar ───────────────────────────────────

fn scalar_box(tid: TypeId, bits: u64) -> AnyBox {
    return AnyBox { tid: tid, form: ANYBOX_SCALAR, code: 0u64, bits: bits, arena: writ_new(0i64) };
}

impl AnyBox {
    /// Boxes a native `i64`. Byte-identical to the box a Writ `WA_I56` Pod or a
    /// boxed `W_I64` normalises to.
    pub fn from(v: i64)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<i64>(),  v as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `u64` (the payload type a Writ `W_U64` normalises to).
    pub fn from(v: u64)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<u64>(),  v); }
    /// Boxes a native `bool` (Writ `WA_BOOL`).
    pub fn from(v: bool) -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<bool>(), if v { 1u64 } else { 0u64 }); }
    /// Boxes a native `f64` (Writ `W_F64`); stored as its IEEE bit pattern.
    pub fn from(v: f64)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<f64>(),  f64_to_bits(v)); }
    /// Boxes a native `f32` (Writ `W_F32`); stored as its IEEE bit pattern in the low 4 bytes.
    pub fn from(v: f32)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<f32>(),  f32_to_bits(v) as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `i8` (Writ `WT_I8`), sign-extended into the inline slot.
    pub fn from(v: i8)   -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<i8>(),   (v as i64) as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `u8` (Writ `WT_U8`).
    pub fn from(v: u8)   -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<u8>(),   v as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `i16` (Writ `WT_I16`), sign-extended into the inline slot.
    pub fn from(v: i16)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<i16>(),  (v as i64) as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `u16` (Writ `WT_U16`).
    pub fn from(v: u16)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<u16>(),  v as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `i24` (Writ `WT_I24`), sign-extended into the inline slot.
    pub fn from(v: i24)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<i24>(),  (v as i64) as u64); }
    /// Boxes a native `u24` (Writ `WT_U24`).
    pub fn from(v: u24)  -> AnyBox { return scalar_box(type_id_of::<u24>(),  (v as i64) as u64); }
}

// ── construction from a Writ value ────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Boxes the Writ value `v` by CLONING it out of its source arena into a fresh arena
/// the box owns — the source may drop immediately after.
///
/// A Writ SCALAR is normalised to its native Logos type (the arena is then unused);
/// anything else is deep-copied and reachable via `as_writ()`. Returns the walker's
/// [CloneRefusal] verbatim when the value cannot be copied — nesting past
/// `COMPACTIFY_MAX_DEPTH`, an arena tag the walker does not know, or OOM. It never
/// answers a silent null: "I could not copy it" and "it was null" are distinct here,
/// which is why this path uses `try_clone` and not the total-signature `clone`.
pub fn of_writ(v: WAny) -> Result<AnyBox, CloneRefusal> {
    return of_writ_in(v, ANYBOX_SEG);
}

/// [of_writ] with an explicit segment size for the arena the box will own. Use it
/// when the document's size is known — one segment beats a chain of them.
pub fn of_writ_in(v: WAny, seg_size: i64) -> Result<AnyBox, CloneRefusal> {
    let tc: u64 = v.type_code();

    // ── SCALARS: normalise. The Writ code is discarded on purpose — see the header.
    if v.is_pod() {
        if tc == (WA_I56 as u64)  { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_i64())); }
        if tc == (WA_BOOL as u64) { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_bool())); }
        if tc == (WT_I8 as u64)   { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_i8())); }
        if tc == (WT_U8 as u64)   { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_u8())); }
        if tc == (WT_I16 as u64)  { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_i16())); }
        if tc == (WT_U16 as u64)  { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_u16())); }
        if tc == (WT_I24 as u64)  { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_i24())); }
        if tc == (WT_U24 as u64)  { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_u24())); }
        // An inline Pod carrying a code outside the encoding's own table: refuse
        // rather than guess a payload type from a tag nothing defines.
        return Result::Err(CloneRefusal { kind: CLONE_REFUSED_UNKNOWN_TAG, tag: tc });
    }
    if tc == W_I64 { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_i64())); }
    if tc == W_U64 { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_u64())); }
    if tc == W_F32 { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_f32())); }
    if tc == W_F64 { return Result::Ok(AnyBox::from(v.as_f64())); }

    // ── AGGREGATES + null: own the arena, own the value.
    let tid: TypeId = writ_payload_type(tc);
    let h: Writ = writ_new(seg_size);
    match try_clone(&h, v) {
        Result::Ok(c)  => { h.set_root(c); }
        Result::Err(e) => { return Result::Err(e); }
    }
    return Result::Ok(AnyBox { tid: tid, form: ANYBOX_WRIT, code: tc, bits: 0u64, arena: h });
}

// The payload type for a Writ code that stays in the arena: the view type when one
// exists, else the reserved structural marker.
fn writ_payload_type(tc: u64) -> TypeId {
    if tc == W_STRING { return type_id_of::<WString>(); }
    if tc == W_ARRAY  { return type_id_of::<WArray<WAny>>(); }
    if tc == W_MAP    { return type_id_of::<WMap<WString, WAny>>(); }
    return type_id_of::<WritOpaque>();
}

// ── the consumer surface ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

impl AnyBox {
    /// Returns the `TypeId` of the PAYLOAD — never of the box, and never of a Writ
    /// wrapper. A box over a Writ `i64` reports `type_id_of::<i64>()`.
    pub fn type_id(self: &AnyBox) -> TypeId { return self.tid; }

    /// Returns whether the payload is a `_T` — the `(dyn Any).is::<T>()` analogue.
    pub fn holds<_T>(self: &AnyBox) -> bool {
        let want: TypeId = type_id_of::<_T>();
        return self.tid.eq(&want);
    }

    /// Recovers `&T` when the payload is a `T`, else `None`.
    ///
    /// The `&T` is borrow-tied to `&self`: for a scalar it points at the box's own
    /// inline slot, for a Writ payload at the object inside the arena the box owns.
    /// Either way it cannot outlive the box, so there is no provenance question.
    ///
    /// ⚠ `T` MUST BE SIZED. Instantiating this at a DST — `WString` is the one in
    /// reach — forms `Option<&WString>`, i.e. an `Option` niche over a FAT pointer,
    /// and the compiler's layout engines disagree about that type: `layout_of` says
    /// 16, `mono_abi_layout` says 24, and the verifier aborts the compile ("a value
    /// would be written at one stride and read at another"), taking 24 iterator
    /// adaptors over it down with it. MEASURED PRE-EXISTING on the untouched
    /// main-repo build from a 20-line program that only uses shipped stdlib
    /// (/home/logos/sandbox/probe/optdst.logos: 24 disagreements; the same program
    /// with the `Option<&WString>` removed, optdst2.logos: 0). Nothing in this
    /// package causes it and nothing here can fix it — the DST payload therefore has
    /// its own accessor, [wstring_ptr], which never forms the broken shape.
    ///
    /// ⚠ RE-MEASURED 2026-08-06 AND CONFIRMED, after a review pass reported it did
    /// NOT reproduce and nearly had the accessor deleted. That review's probe — and
    /// a second one run independently — compiled the FIXTURE with
    /// `downcast_ref::<WString>()` against a stdlib that STILL CONTAINED
    /// `wstring_ptr`, so the DST `Option` existed only in the test object and the
    /// instantiation set stayed small. Remove the accessor from the stdlib as well
    /// and the compile ABORTS:
    ///   [product] logos.lang.iter.EnumIter$G2$OptionIter$G1$dstref_WString$...:
    ///     mono_abi_layout says 32, llvm::DataLayout says 24
    /// `FilterIter` the same; 24 disagreements in total. The broken shape is reached
    /// through the ITERATOR machinery over `Option<&WString>`, which is exactly why
    /// a probe that only calls `downcast_ref` directly comes back clean.
    ///
    /// THE METHOD POINT, since this cost two independent wrong measurements: a
    /// workaround can only be tested for necessity by REMOVING IT FROM WHERE IT
    /// LIVES and rebuilding everything downstream. Substituting at the call site
    /// while the workaround still stands measures nothing.
    pub fn downcast_ref<T>(self: &AnyBox) -> Option<&T> {
        let want: TypeId = type_id_of::<T>();
        if !self.tid.eq(&want) { return Option::None; }
        if self.form == ANYBOX_SCALAR {
            return Option::Some(unsafe { &*(&self.bits as *const u64 as *const T) });
        }
        return Option::Some(unsafe { &*(self.arena.root().resolve() as *const T) });
    }

    /// Returns `true` iff the payload stayed a Writ value (an aggregate or an opaque
    /// tagged object) rather than normalising to a native scalar.
    pub fn is_writ(self: &AnyBox) -> bool { return self.form == ANYBOX_WRIT; }

    /// Returns `true` iff the payload is a Writ string.
    pub fn is_wstring(self: &AnyBox) -> bool {
        return self.form == ANYBOX_WRIT && self.code == W_STRING;
    }

    /// Returns a pointer to the `WString` payload inside the box's own arena, or
    /// NULL when the payload is not a Writ string. Valid while `self` lives.
    ///
    /// A raw pointer, not `Option<&WString>`, and not by choice: `WString` is a DST,
    /// so `Option<&WString>` is an `Option` niche over a fat pointer — the shape the
    /// layout engines disagree about (see [downcast_ref]). Null-on-mismatch keeps
    /// the function TOTAL without forming that type. `holds::<WString>()` still
    /// reports the payload type, so the `TypeId` surface is unaffected.
    pub fn wstring_ptr(self: &AnyBox) -> *const WString {
        if !self.is_wstring() { return 0i64 as *const WString; }
        return self.arena.root().resolve() as *const WString;
    }

    /// Returns the originating Writ wire code for a Writ payload — the exact
    /// discriminator for a `WritOpaque` one. 0 for a normalised scalar, whatever
    /// Writ code it arrived as: the box does not remember, by design.
    pub fn writ_code(self: &AnyBox) -> u64 { return self.code; }

    /// Returns the Writ payload as a `WAny` rooted in the box's own arena, or the
    /// null `WAny` for a scalar payload (pair it with [is_writ]).
    ///
    /// The handle is only valid while the box lives — it is a borrow of the arena
    /// the box owns, so do not outlive `self` with it.
    pub fn as_writ(self: &AnyBox) -> WAny {
        if self.form != ANYBOX_WRIT { return WAny::null(); }
        return self.arena.root();
    }
}
